{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>If a bird's nest happens to be before you</b>, this too is an explicit commandment, like <b>it and its young you shall not slaughter on the same day</b> (Leviticus 22:28), for the reason in both of them is so that we not have a cruel heart and lack compassion, or that Scripture did not permit making destruction to eradicate the species, even though it permitted slaughter of that species. And one who kills the mother and the young on one day, or takes them when they have freedom to fly, it is as though he eradicates that species. And the Rav wrote in the Guide for the Perplexed (3:48) that the reason for sending away the mother bird and the reason for <b>it and its young you shall not slaughter on the same day</b> is to warn lest one slaughter the young in the eyes of the mother, for animals have great distress over this, and there is no difference between the distress of a human and the distress of animals over their young, for the mother's love and tenderness for the offspring of her womb does not follow from the intellect and speech but is an effect of the faculty of thought found in animals just as it is found in humans. If so, the essence of the prohibition in <b>it and its young</b> is not specifically [in the order of] its young and then it, but everything is [a matter of] distance [from cruelty]. And the more correct [reason] is for the sake of our not becoming cruel. And the Rav said: do not object to me from the saying of the Sages (Berakhot 33a), 'One who says [concerning] a bird's nest, may Your mercy reach it' — for this is one of two positions, the position of one who holds that the commandments have no reason but only the will of the Creator, whereas we maintain the second position, that there should be in all the commandments a reason. And an objection was further raised against him from what is found in Bereshit Rabbah (44:1): 'Now what does it matter to HaShem whether one slaughters from the neck or slaughters from the nape? Rather, the commandments were given only to refine the creatures through them, as it is stated (Proverbs 30:5), every word of El is refined.' And this notion that the Rav decreed concerning the commandments — that they have a reason — is very well clarified, for in each one there is a reason and a benefit and a rectification for a person, apart from their reward from the One who commanded them, blessed be He. And our Sages already said (Sanhedrin 21b): for what reason were the reasons of the Torah not revealed, etc. And they interpreted (Pesahim 119a): <b>and to conceal [עתיק] a matter</b> — this refers to one who reveals matters that the Ancient of Days [עתיק יומין] concealed; and what are these? The reasons of the Torah. And they already interpreted concerning the red heifer (Bemidbar Rabbah 19:3): that Solomon said, 'I have observed all things, and the section of the red heifer I searched and inquired and scrutinized; I said I will grow wise, but it is far from me' (Ecclesiastes 7:23). And Rabbi Yosei ben Rabbi Hanina said: HaShem said to Moses, to you I reveal the reason of the red heifer, but to others it is a statute, as it is written (Zechariah 14:6), <b>and it shall come to pass on that day, there shall not be light; precious ones [יקרות] congeal [וקפאון]</b> — <b>וקפאון</b> is read <b>יקפאון</b> (things congealed/frozen): matters that are hidden from you in this world are destined to be visible in the world to come, like this blind person who sees, as it is written (Isaiah 42:16), <b>and I will lead the blind in a way they did not know</b>, and it is written (ibid.), <b>these things I have done and have not forsaken them</b> — I have already done them for Rabbi Akiva. Behold, they explained that the concealment of the reasons of the Torah from us is nothing but blindness in our intellect, and the reason for the most stringent among them was already revealed to the sages of Israel, and there are many like these in their words, and in the Torah and in Scripture many things indicate so, and the Rav mentioned some of them. But these aggadot that posed difficulty for the Rav have, in my opinion, a different intent, for they wished to say that the commandments carry no benefit for HaShem Himself, exalted be He, but the benefit is for man himself — to prevent harm from him, or false belief, or a despicable trait, or to recall the miracles and wonders of the Creator blessed be He and to know HaShem. And this is <b>to refine through them</b>, that they be like refined silver, for the refiner of silver does not do his work without reason, but to remove all dross from it; and so the commandments are to remove from our heart every false belief, to make known to us the truth, and to remember always. And this very language of the aggadah is cited in the Tanchuma (Shemini 8) in the section of <b>these are the creatures</b>: 'Now what does it matter to HaShem whether one slaughters an animal and eats, or stabs [נוחר] and eats — do you perhaps benefit Him, or do you perhaps harm Him? Or what does it matter to Him whether one eats pure [creatures] or eats impure [creatures]? <b>If you are wise, you are wise for yourself</b> (Proverbs 9:12) — rather, the commandments were given only to refine the creatures, as it is stated (Psalms 12:7), <b>the words of YHWH are pure words</b>, and it is stated, <b>every word of El is refined</b>. Why? <b>That it may be a shield for you</b>.' Behold it is explicitly explained here that they came to say only that the benefit is not for Him, exalted be He — that He should need its light like [what is] taken from the lamp [of the menorah], and that He should need the eating of the sacrifices and the fragrance of the incense as would appear from their plain sense — and even the remembrance of His wonders that He commanded to do as a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt and the work of Creation, the benefit is not for Him, but rather that we should know the truth and merit through it until we become worthy of being a shield over ourselves, for our speech and our remembrance of His wonders are reckoned to Him as nothing and void. And he brought proof from one who slaughters from the neck or the nape, to say that all of them are for us and not for HaShem, because it is impossible to say regarding slaughter that there should be in it benefit and glory for the Creator blessed be He at the neck more than the nape or stabbing, but rather they are for us, to guide us in the paths of mercy even at the time of slaughter. And they brought another proof: 'or what does it matter to Him whether one eats pure [creatures],' and they are the permitted foods, 'or eats impure [creatures],' and they are the forbidden foods, which the Torah called (Leviticus 11:28) <b>they are impure for you</b> — and He hinted that it is so that we may be pure of soul, wise, and discerners of truth. And their saying <b>if you are wise, you are wise for yourself</b> — they intimated that the practical commandments, such as slaughter at the neck, are to teach us good traits, and the decreatory commandments demarcated by species are to purify our souls, as the Torah said (Leviticus 20:25), <b>and you shall not make your souls detestable through beast or bird or anything that creeps on the earth, which I have separated for you as impure</b>. If so, all of them are for our benefit alone. And this is as Elihu said (Job 35:6), <b>if you sin, what do you effect against Him, and if your transgressions abound, what do you do to Him?</b>, and he said (ibid. v. 7), <b>or what does He take from your hand?</b>. And this is agreed upon in all the words of our Sages. And they asked in the Jerusalem Talmud in Nedarim (9:1): may one open [a conversation] with a person concerning the honor of HaShem in matters between him and HaShem? And they answered this question: what is [meant by] the honor of HaShem? Such as sukkah that I do not make, lulav that I do not take, tefillin that I do not don — and that is the honor of HaShem. This implies that it benefits Him for His own sake, as in, <b>if you are righteous, what do you give Him, or what does He take from your hand? If you sin, what do you effect against Him, and if your transgressions abound, what do you do to Him?</b> Behold they explained that even the lulav and the sukkah and the tefillin that He commanded as a sign on your hand and a remembrance between your eyes that <b>with a strong hand YHWH brought you out of Egypt</b> — they are not for the honor of HaShem blessed be He, but to have compassion on our souls. And they already arranged for us in the prayer of Yom Kippur: 'You distinguished man from the beginning and acknowledged him to stand before You, for who can say to You what You do, and if he is righteous, what does he give You?' And thus the Torah says (Deuteronomy 10:13), <b>for your good</b>, as I explained (ibid. v. 12). And likewise, <b>and YHWH commanded us to do all these statutes, to fear HaShem our Elohim, for our good all the days</b> (Deuteronomy 6:24), and the intent in all of them is for our good and not for Him, blessed and exalted be He. Rather, everything we were commanded is so that His creatures be refined and purified, without the dross of evil thoughts and despicable traits. And likewise what they said (Berakhot 33a): 'because one makes the attributes of HaShem [into] mercy, yet they are nothing but decrees' — to say that HaShem has no pity on a bird's nest, and His mercy does not reach <b>it and its young</b>, for His mercy does not extend to possessors of an animal soul to prevent us from doing our needs with them; for if so, He would have forbidden slaughter. But the reason for the prohibition is to teach us the trait of mercy and so that we not become cruel, for cruelty spreads in a person's soul, as is known among the slaughterers who slaughter large cattle and donkeys, that they are men of blood, sacrificers of men, very cruel. And because of this they said (Kiddushin 82a): 'The best among slaughterers is a partner of Amalek.' And these commandments concerning animals and birds are not mercy upon them, but decrees upon us, to guide us and to teach us good traits. And thus they call all the commandments in the Torah, positive and negative, 'decrees,' as they said (Mekhilta, Bahodesh 6) in the parable of a king who entered a province: his servants said to him, decree decrees upon them! He said to them: when they accept my sovereignty, I will decree decrees upon them. So said HaShem: you accepted my sovereignty — <b>I am YHWH your Elohim</b> (Exodus 20:2); accept my decrees — <b>you shall have no [other gods]</b> (ibid. v. 3). But in the midrash of Rabbi Nehunya ben HaKanah on the sending away of the nest, which is a midrash [holding] that there is a mystery [סוד] in the commandment, Rabbi Rahmai said: what is written, <b>you shall surely send away the mother</b> — and it does not say the father, but <b>you shall surely send away the mother</b> — in honor of that discernment [בינה] which is called the mother of the world, as it is written (Proverbs 2:3), <b>if you call out to understanding [לבינה]</b>. What is <b>and the young you shall take for yourself</b>? Rabbi Rahmai said: those children that it [the binah] raised — and what are they? The seven days of Sukkot, and the laws of the seven days of the week, etc. And this commandment alludes to a great matter, and therefore its reward is great: <b>that it may go well with you and that you may prolong your days</b>."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:45.011629399Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}